🎯 Strategy Design Pattern
📌 Definition

👉 Strategy Pattern allows you to define a family of algorithms, put each of them in separate classes, and make them 
interchangeable at runtime.

In simple words:

You can change behavior (strategy) of an object without changing its code.

🧠 Think Like a 10-Year-Old

Imagine you are going to college:

Sometimes you go by Bike 🏍️
Sometimes by Bus 🚌
Sometimes by Walking 🚶

👉 Destination is same
👉 Only strategy (way of travel) changes

That’s Strategy Pattern.

❌ Without Strategy Pattern (Bad Design)
class Travel {
    void go(String mode) {
        if (mode.equals("bike")) {
            System.out.println("Going by bike");
        } else if (mode.equals("bus")) {
            System.out.println("Going by bus");
        }
    }
}
🚨 Problem:
Every new mode → modify class
Violates Open/Closed Principle
Not scalable
✅ With Strategy Pattern (Good Design)
Step 1: Create Strategy Interface
interface TravelStrategy {
    void travel();
}
Step 2: Create Different Strategies
class BikeStrategy implements TravelStrategy {
    public void travel() {
        System.out.println("Travel by Bike");
    }
}

class BusStrategy implements TravelStrategy {
    public void travel() {
        System.out.println("Travel by Bus");
    }
}

class WalkStrategy implements TravelStrategy {
    public void travel() {
        System.out.println("Travel by Walking");
    }
}
Step 3: Context Class
class Navigator {
    private TravelStrategy strategy;

    Navigator(TravelStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    void setStrategy(TravelStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    void startJourney() {
        strategy.travel();
    }
}
Step 4: Usage
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Navigator nav = new Navigator(new BikeStrategy());
        nav.startJourney();

        nav.setStrategy(new BusStrategy());
        nav.startJourney();
    }
}
✅ Result:
Behavior changes at runtime
No modification of existing code
Clean & scalable
🌍 Real-World Applications
1️⃣ Payment System 💳
Strategy = Payment Method
UPI / Card / Wallet
2️⃣ Sorting Algorithms
Strategy = sorting logic
QuickSort / MergeSort
3️⃣ Navigation Apps 🗺️
Strategy = route
Fastest / Shortest / No toll
🧠 When to Use Strategy Pattern?

Use it when:

✔ You have multiple ways to perform a task
✔ You see many if-else or switch cases
✔ You want runtime flexibility
✔ You want to follow Open/Closed Principle

⚙️ Benefits
✅ Removes if-else clutter
✅ Easy to extend
✅ Runtime flexibility
✅ Cleaner code
⚠️ Drawback
❌ More number of classes
❌ Slight complexity increase
🧱 Composition Over Inheritance (Very Important)
📌 Definition

👉 Favor using objects (composition) instead of inheriting classes to reuse behavior.

In simple words:

Instead of saying “is-a”, prefer “has-a”

🧠 Simple Intuition
Inheritance

👉 Dog is-a Animal

Composition

👉 Car has-a Engine

❌ Problem with Inheritance
class Bird {
    void fly() { }
}

class Penguin extends Bird { } // ❌ problem

👉 Penguin cannot fly
👉 Breaks Liskov Substitution Principle

✅ Solution using Composition
interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

class FlyWithWings implements Flyable {
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Flying with wings");
    }
}

class Bird {
    Flyable flyBehavior;

    Bird(Flyable flyBehavior) {
        this.flyBehavior = flyBehavior;
    }

    void performFly() {
        flyBehavior.fly();
    }
}
Usage
Bird sparrow = new Bird(new FlyWithWings());
sparrow.performFly();

👉 Now behavior is flexible

🎯 Why Composition Over Inheritance?
1️⃣ Flexibility

👉 Change behavior at runtime

2️⃣ Avoid Tight Coupling

👉 Classes are independent

3️⃣ Follows SOLID
✔ OCP
✔ LSP
✔ DIP
4️⃣ Reusability

👉 Same behavior reused in multiple classes

⚡ Key Difference
Inheritance	Composition
IS-A relationship	HAS-A relationship
Tight coupling	Loose coupling
Fixed behavior	Flexible behavior
Compile-time	Runtime change possible
🔥 Strategy Pattern = Composition in Action

👉 Strategy pattern is a perfect example of composition

Navigator HAS-A TravelStrategy

👉 That’s why:

Behavior can change dynamically
Code stays clean
🎯 Interview One-Liner

👉 “Strategy pattern uses composition to allow selecting algorithms at runtime instead of hardcoding them using inheritance.”

⚡ Memory Trick

👉 Strategy = Change behavior easily
👉 Composition = Plug & Play behavior